00:07:49.819 --> 00:07:56.539
Second, as I understand it, the hashes that
are used right here aren't like classic cryptographic

00:07:56.539 --> 00:07:57.539
hashes.

00:07:57.539 --> 00:08:02.699
They are what Apple calls neural hash, but
what is in effect, a locality sensitive hashing

00:08:02.699 --> 00:08:03.699
algorithm.

00:08:03.699 --> 00:08:09.199
So here's an article by Tyler neilan, about
locality sensitive hashing, which explains

00:08:09.199 --> 00:08:11.219
the concept fairly well.

00:08:11.219 --> 00:08:16.229
And it makes sense to use a locality sensitive
hash in this case, because what you want to

00:08:16.229 --> 00:08:21.639
detect is if two images are the same meaning
display the same thing.

00:08:21.639 --> 00:08:27.050
For example, if I take an image and then run
some sort of JPEG compression on it, it still

00:08:27.050 --> 00:08:28.339
shows me the same thing.

00:08:28.339 --> 00:08:30.180
However, the bits have all changed.

00:08:30.180 --> 00:08:33.769
So a classic hash would not be able to recognize
that image anymore.

00:08:33.769 --> 00:08:39.339
However, a Content Aware hash would or should
at least be able to recognize that this is

00:08:39.339 --> 00:08:44.730
the same image YouTube has been doing this
for a long time with their content ID system,

00:08:44.730 --> 00:08:49.410
detecting when someone re uploads a video
by someone else, even if that video has been

00:08:49.410 --> 00:08:50.459
re encoded.

00:08:50.459 --> 00:08:54.561
So as far as I understand it, what Apple does
is they train some kind of neural network

00:08:54.561 --> 00:08:58.420
that gives them a representation of what is
in an image.

00:08:58.420 --> 00:09:02.190
And then they run that through a locality
sensitive hashing procedure.

00:09:02.190 --> 00:09:07.410
locality sensitive hashing is essentially
a system that allows you to find neighbors

00:09:07.410 --> 00:09:10.759
in very high dimensional space very efficiently.

00:09:10.759 --> 00:09:16.420
So the neural network would produce a space
of images and place each image somewhere with

00:09:16.420 --> 00:09:22.670
the intention that images containing similar
or the same thing would fall very close to

00:09:22.670 --> 00:09:23.670
each other.

00:09:23.670 --> 00:09:25.030
And you can do that with neural network.

00:09:25.030 --> 00:09:28.800
The question is, you don't want to run an
inner product search over this whole space

00:09:28.800 --> 00:09:31.829
all the time, like that would fry your phone,
probably.

00:09:31.829 --> 00:09:38.160
So what locality sensitive hashing does, essentially,
it divides up this space into buckets.

00:09:38.160 --> 00:09:40.440
So here, it's straight buckets.

00:09:40.440 --> 00:09:42.190
And then these kinds of buckets.

00:09:42.190 --> 00:09:46.350
Once you combine all these buckets, you get
the sub bucket, so you get sort of a division

00:09:46.350 --> 00:09:53.190
of space and for each point you can check
is it to the left or to the right of a particular

00:09:53.190 --> 00:09:54.190
line.

00:09:54.190 --> 00:09:59.720
And if two points match in being to the left
or to the right, or up or down respectively,

00:09:59.720 --> 00:10:04.120
towards Any particular line, that means they're
in the same bucket and probably very close

00:10:04.120 --> 00:10:09.230
together at that point, then you can actually
go ahead and check if they are actually close

00:10:09.230 --> 00:10:10.320
together or not.

00:10:10.320 --> 00:10:15.199
This is a good way to find approximately nearest
neighbors in high dimensions.

00:10:15.199 --> 00:10:18.620
So real LSH algorithms are a bit more sophisticated.

00:10:18.620 --> 00:10:21.050
But that's the essential concept they work
by.

